.TH CJSON_DESTROY 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cjson_destroy \- free a DOM tree and NULL the handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

cjson_destroy(node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Frees node together with every descendant reachable from it, then sets
node to NULL. A node that is already NULL is left alone and nothing is
freed.

The macro destroys the node and then assigns NULL to node. This is
why node must be an assignable lvalue of type cjson, and not any
expression. Application code must use this form. The assignment of
NULL is what makes an accidental second destroy of the same variable
a no-op instead of a double free.

The macro frees each node through the allocator that created it.
That is the allocator that the caller gave to the
cjson_create_*_mp() or cjson_parse_mp() call which made the node.
This is why the macro needs no allocator argument.

The macro tears the subtree down without one recursion for each
level. It therefore destroys a document of any depth and never
exhausts the call stack. This is also true for a document that the
caller builds directly with cjson_list_push() and
cjson_dictionary_set(), which have no depth bound at all.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
node  Assignable cjson variable naming the root of the subtree to
      free. Set to NULL on return. A NULL variable is a no-op. Must
      name a tree the caller owns, that is, one that is not currently
      attached to a parent container.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to a statement, not to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro evaluates node one time only. node must be a modifiable
lvalue, for example a variable or an element of an array. Therefore, an
expression with a side effect, for example handles[i++], destroys and
clears one element.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
A node that cjson_dictionary_get(), cjson_list_get() or cjson_get()
returns is a borrowed reference into a tree. The caller does not own
it, because the parent still owns it. A destroy of such a node frees
it while the parent still points at it. A destroy of the parent
after that is a double free. Destroy only a root that the caller
owns. To remove a child, call cjson_dictionary_remove() or
cjson_list_remove(), which already free what they remove.

A node that the caller gives to cjson_list_push() or
cjson_dictionary_set() belongs to the container from that moment.
On most failure paths, the node also belongs to the container,
because the call deep-freed it. A separate destroy of the node is then
a double free. Only ccol_invalid_args leaves the node with the caller,
unchanged. These cases give ccol_invalid_args:
  - A container that is NULL or of the wrong kind.
  - A node that is attached to a container.
  - A node that is the container itself.
  - An attach that would make a cycle.
  - For cjson_dictionary_set(), a key that is NULL or not valid UTF-8.
All other failures take ownership and free the node. This includes
each ccol_not_enough_memory. cjson_list_push(3) gives the exact split
of ownership.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cjson doc = cjson_parse("{\e"k\e":[1,2,3]}", NULL);
if (!doc) return;

/* ... use doc ... */

cjson_destroy(doc);   /* doc is NULL afterwards */
cjson_destroy(doc);   /* harmless no-op */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_declare_scoped (3),
.BR cjson_parse (3),
.BR cjson_clone (3),
.BR cjson_delete (3),
.BR cjson_list_push (3)
